course: dictionary
lesson: 14259
title: Neutralized: made harmless
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Neutralized is a verb. It means stopped from having its effect, or made harmless. Look at the clues around it: what was the problem, and what happened to it? «The baking soda neutralized the acid in the spilled vinegar.» The acid was the problem, and after the baking soda it could no longer hurt anything. So neutralized means made harmless.
explain: In science, an acid is neutralized by an alkali, which is its opposite. «The indigestion tablet neutralized the acid in Sam's stomach.» Ask yourself: what was the problem, and what stopped it? Then try your meaning in the sentence. "The tablet made the acid harmless" still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Neutralized can also describe everyday problems. «The fresh air neutralized the smell of the paint.» The smell was still there, but it no longer bothered anyone. In stories and news, neutralized can mean that a threat was stopped from doing harm. Always check the context to see which meaning fits.
fact: Neutralized is the past tense of the verb neutralize, and it can also be used as an adjective.
fact: «Neutralized» means made harmless, or stopped from having its effect.
fact: In science, an acid is neutralized by a base, such as an alkali. Vinegar and baking soda is a classic example.
fact: UK spelling is neutralised, but the meaning is the same.
fact: In military or police talk, a threat that has been neutralized is unable to do harm.
exercise: «The baking soda neutralized the acid in the spilled vinegar.» What does neutralized mean here? | Made louder and stronger | Frozen solid | Spread all over the table | *Made harmless, so the acid can no longer hurt
exercise: «The alarm rang, but the guard neutralized the threat quickly.» What did the guard do to the threat? | Made it bigger | Ran away from it | *Stopped it from doing harm | Painted it bright red
exercise: «The fresh air neutralized the smell of the paint, so the room felt nice again.» Find and copy the word that means made harmless. | paint | room | fresh | *neutralized
test: Ella says neutralized means made stronger. Is she right? | Yes, it means made stronger, like a battery | *No, it means made harmless or stopped from having its effect | Not sure, so she should guess | No, it means made into a new colour
test: «The police neutralized the bomb.» What does this sentence mean? | Set the bomb off with a loud bang | Took a photo of the bomb | *Made the bomb unable to do harm | Sold the bomb to a shop
exam: «The treatment plant neutralized the acid before the water went into the river.» What does neutralized mean here? | Turned into a river | Frozen solid | *Made harmless by mixing in an opposite | Made hotter and faster
